### Page 6 of 14

```text
PLN-22-00010

October 3, 2022
ANALYSIS OF APPLICATION CONFORMANCE WITH REVIEW CRITERIA:

Should the Planning Commission wish to approve an amendment to the Future Land Use Map of the
Comprehensive Plan, the Commission must make the following considerations outlined in the 2014
Community Plan (page 18).  A detailed analysis is below:

1) BENEFICIAL OR ADDRESSES ISSUES - The proposed amendment is beneficial to the community
or addresses issues not foreseen during the planning process.

The proposed future land use map amendment at this location is beneficial to the community
and addresses an issue not foreseen during the planning process for the 2014 PlanCheyenne
amendment or the Connect 2045 amendment. When these were adopted, it was not known
that the subject property had the potential for limited industrial development. However, the
market and property owners noted the prime location of the subject property on the periphery
of the community; the large amount of space available; and proximity to I-25, the railroad, and
South Greeley Highway as a logical location for more intensive development. The development
at this location also warrants an amendment of the street network shown on the map. This
amendment will facilitate development in South Cheyenne and further several economic
development goals contained within PlanCheyenne.

The proposed Future Land Use amendment complies with review criterion 1.

2) COMPATIBLY - The proposed amendment is compatible with the surrounding area, and the
goals and policies of the Plan.

The subject property is located in an area that is largely undeveloped, with the exception of the
residential development located to the east and southeast. Due to its isolated nature, the
subject property is suitable for a mix of limited industrial and commercial development, noting
the impacts of the limited industrial development will be minimal to the largely vacant
surrounding properties. The proposed street network in Connect 2045 will need to be modified
to go around this development, but all edges of this development can be serviced by the
regional network. The proposed amendment will further various economic development
objectives contained within PlanCheyenne, specifically the policies contained in Principle 3.5:
Provide Opportunities for a Range of Industrial Employment Uses.

The proposed Future Land Use amendment complies with review criteria 2.

3) IMPACT MITIGATION - The proposed amendment will address and mitigate impacts on
transportation, services, and facilities.

Transportation:
The transportation network in this area of the City is largely undeveloped. As High Plains
Business Park develops, the property owners and developers will be required to dedicate
adequate right-of-way to service the business park and mitigate the development’s impact on
existing rights-of-way. Additional rights-of-way are proposed to be constructed with this
development.
```

### Page 7 of 14

```text
PLN-22-00010

October 3, 2022
Services:
The proposed change will require coordination with the Board of Public Utilities and Fire
Department through the platting and site plan process. The proposed development will be
required to meet the standards of these reviewing departments and agencies.

Facilities:
The proposed change may require additional facilities as determined by the platting or site plan
processes. These changes will be addressed at these phases of the development process.

The proposed Future Land Use amendment complies with review criteria 3.

4) SERVICE PROVISION - The proposed amendment will have minimal effect on service provision,
including adequacy or availability of urban facilities and services, and is compatible with
existing and planned service provision

The land is currently being annexed by the City of Cheyenne. It is within the Urban Service
Boundary and the development will be required to meet the Board of Public Utilities service
requirements and standards.

The proposed Future Land Use amendment complies with review criteria 4.

5) ANNEXATION - The proposed amendment does not jeopardize the City’s ability to annex the
property.

The property is currently proposed for annexation.

The proposed Future Land Use amendment complies with review criteria 5.
 .
6) URBAN SERVICES AREA - The proposed amendment is consistent with the logical expansion of
the Urban Services Area.

The proposed amendment is within the Urban Services Boundary. The expansion of services at
this location is logical as it is adjacent to current services and assists in the continued
development in South Cheyenne.

The proposed Future Land Use amendment complies with review criteria 6.

7) STRICT ADHERENCE - Strict adherence to the Plan would result in a situation neither intended
nor in keeping with other key elements and policies of the Plan.

This area of the City was proposed as largely developing with residential uses. However, there
were no active proposals, and the designation was a ‘best guess’ as to what would be
appropriate in this location. PlanCheyenne encourages flexibility to respond to market demands
and views the future land use map as a guide. Large acreage parcels on the edge of the
community with access to regional transportation routes are considered optimal for intensive
employment uses. Strict adherence to the Plan may result in unintended consequences. The
proposed Business Park at this location provides an opportunity for furthering Cheyenne’s
position in the regional economy. The business park will result in more employment and tax
revenue generated by the City. Strict adherence to the currently adopted Cheyenne Area Future
```

### Page 8 of 14

```text
PLN-22-00010

October 3, 2022
Land Use & Transportation Map may prohibit the business park from coming to fruition and
result in a missed opportunity for further economic development and diversification.

The proposed Future Land Use amendment complies with review criteria 7.

8) PUBLIC WELFARE AND CONSISTENCY – The proposed plan amendment will promote the public
welfare and will be consistent with the goals and policies of PlanCheyenne and the elements
thereof.

Staff is satisfied that the proposed application will not have any adverse effects on public
welfare. Furthermore, the application accomplishes the following policies contained within
PlanCheyenne:

•
Policy 1.1.B. Flexibility to Market Demands: This goal notes that the future land use
plan does not predetermine all uses on individual parcels and is meant to provide
flexibility. The future land use map amendment and Urban Services Boundary
amendment will serve as an economic development opportunity that was not seen
when the future land use map was adopted.

The proposed Future Land Use amendment complies with review criteria 8.

CONFORMANCE WITH APPLICABLE PLANS:

No other plans are applicable for this area.

CONCLUSION:

Staff recommends approving this Cheyenne Area Future Land Use & Transportation Map
amendment due to the proposal’s positive impact on economic development and diversification
in Cheyenne. The proposed amendment will support future annexations in the area, possibly
creating more jobs, housing, and tax revenue for the City. The proposed development will be
required to work with the City to ensure adequate infrastructure and facilities serve the
business park. Staff is satisfied this amendment meets the review criteria set forth in
PlanCheyenne and is supportive of this request.

ALTERNATIVES:

1. Approve the Resolution amending the Cheyenne Area Future Land Use & Transportation Map
(Staff Recommendation).
2. Approve the Resolution amending the Cheyenne Area Future Land Use & Transportation Map
subject to modifications.
3. Deny the Resolution to not amend the Cheyenne Area Future Land Use & Transportation Map.
The Planning Commission must conclude that the project does not meet all the review criteria.
As part of the motion, findings for denial must be stated.
4. Postpone the Resolution until issues identified during the meeting can be resolved.

General Information Regarding Alternatives:

In the event that the Commission acts contrary to staff’s recommendation, the Commission shall cite specific
reasons (based on the review criteria) for approval or denial.
```

